window.ESSAYS_EN = {
  '1': {
    title: "We Built a New Home",
    body: [
      "Friends, today's essay is not like the usual ones.",
      "For several years now, we have been writing essays on our Telegram channel. The second, the fifth, the fifteenth... each one written with a topic, a thought, a hope. Our project grew there, and there it multiplied.",
      "But there was one problem I always felt.",
      "Telegram is a platform built for quick messages. Someone writes to you, \"Hi, are we meeting today?\" and you answer, \"Yes.\" An exchange of a few seconds. For long texts, Telegram is an awkward place. You scroll and scroll and keep on scrolling. You lose track of where it began and where it ends. The screen is small, the letters are crowded, and your eyes get tired.",
      "Our essays, on the other hand, are long. Sometimes they run past two thousand words. To open up an idea fully, to enrich it with examples, to bring in evidence from history, from science, from life, you need room.",
      "That is why we decided to build a new home.",
      "This website is here to solve exactly that problem: to present long essays in a way that is comfortable to read, easy on the eyes, and where you won't get lost between pages.",
      "Our Telegram channel will, of course, carry on. That is where you'll find short thoughts, announcements, and a notice whenever a new essay comes out. But the essays themselves, their main home, will now be here.",
      "Why does this step matter?",
      "Because writing is not a temporary thing. I want the fifth essay, and the fiftieth too, to still be read years from now. On Telegram, an old message is soon buried. A new member of the channel gets lost, from their very first day, among a hundred and fifty or so essays. Here, there is order. There are titles. There are dates. There is a way to search.",
      "This is an archive, for you and for me alike. A home for the thoughts we have written over the years.",
      "I felt it was right to make this announcement the first essay. Because every new beginning starts by explaining why it began.",
      "Welcome to our new home."
    ].join('\n\n')
  },
  '2': {
    title: "You Can't Judge a Whole Tree by a Single Leaf",
    body: [
      "Friends, today I want to share an observation of mine with you.",
      "I watch conversations. I read the comments on social media. And I have noticed one thing that keeps repeating: people simplify complicated situations and lump everything together. In English this is called generalization.",
      "A man whose marriage didn't work out declares all women ungrateful. A woman whose marriage didn't work out accuses all men of unfaithfulness and says, \"All men are like that,\" as if she had been in a relationship with every one of the four billion men on earth, and all 4,000,000,000 of them had found the time to cheat on her.",
      "A person makes a mistake once, and conclusions are drawn about both their past and their future. The verdict is handed down that they will make the same mistake again. But judging is not our place, is it? There is only One who judges. Let us not forget who the Owner of hearts is.",
      "Because many Uzbeks go to Russia for manual labor, people say, \"All Uzbeks are like that: illiterate, a nation of laborers.\" In Korea, one Uzbek drives badly and causes a person's death. From that, a conclusion is drawn about the whole nation: so Uzbeks are bad drivers.",
      "If two people from the same family see their marriages fall apart, people say, \"See? That family must have raised its children badly.\" They don't even stop to consider how many reasons can lie behind a divorce. It doesn't occur to them that both partners may have been raised well and simply had characters that didn't fit. Maybe they were raised badly, maybe they weren't. But the reasons are always individual.",
      "You can see this in many other places too. If one young man drifts through life aimlessly, people say, \"That's how young people are these days.\" If one doctor makes a mistake, they say, \"You can't trust doctors.\" The mistake of one follower gets pinned on his entire school of thought (*mazhab*). If the eldest child turns out badly, people say, \"That's how this family's children are,\" and look at the younger one with suspicion too. If someone from a certain region steals, they say, \"The people of such-and-such region are all thieves,\" or \"People from such-and-such region are thieves by nature.\" If a rich man is arrogant, they say, \"That's how the rich are.\" If a poor man doesn't work, they say, \"The poor are lazy.\" If one person who calls himself a Muslim commits a crime, Muslims get branded as terrorists.",
      "All of these share a single root: what one or two people did is turned into the character of an entire nation or group. Because for the brain, that is the convenient, easy road. \"Judging each person on their own\" is tiring and takes time. Saying \"they're all the same\" gives the brain an easy way out. And ease is often built on a lie.",
      "We also need to watch our words. When we say \"everyone,\" do we really mean everyone? When we say \"most people,\" is there an actual number behind it, or is that just our impression too? If we don't know for sure, saying \"some of them\" is closer to the truth.",
      "The subtlest and most dangerous form of this generalization is mixing up worship with conduct.",
      "If a girl who wears hijab does something wrong, people say, \"So much for girls in hijab,\" and put a label on every woman who wears it. If the sin of someone who prays comes to light, they say, \"So that's what people who pray are like, too.\"",
      "This is confusing two different things with each other.",
      "Prayer is a connection with Allah, an act of worship. Sin is human weakness, a test of one's lower self (*nafs*). One does not cancel the other. If a person who prays commits a sin, it doesn't mean their prayer is false. It only means their nafs has not yet been fully disciplined. The Quran says: prayer keeps one away from evil and indecency (Surah al-Ankabut, 29:45). That is a process that works over time, not a magic button that does its job in an instant.",
      "Hijab is obedience to Allah's command, submission shown through what one wears. If a girl in hijab behaves badly, that does not show that hijab is meaningless. It only shows that she is still struggling in some other area of her life.",
      "Most important of all, these are two separate reckonings. On the Day of Judgment, prayer will be asked about separately, and sin will be asked about separately. One does not take the other's place, but neither does one wipe out the other. Every soul answers for its own deeds. No soul bears the burden of another's sin; this is what the Quran points to.",
      "Why do people mix the two up?",
      "Because the human mind likes to see things whole. It is convenient to stick on a label: \"this person is good\" or \"this person is bad.\" Saying \"this person is strong in this area and still struggling in that one\" is much harder by comparison.",
      "But the truth is this: no one is entirely good or entirely bad. Each of us stands at different levels in different areas. There are people whose prayer is strong but whose patience is weak. There are people whose patience is strong but whose knowledge is thin. There are people whose knowledge is great but whose sincerity is weak. None of these gives you the full verdict on the others.",
      "Generalization brings one more harm: it is often used to justify ourselves. The person who says \"all men are like that\" is trying to explain their own wound. The person who says \"well, people who pray sin too\" is trying to justify not praying themselves. Generalization sometimes turns out to be an attempt to soften the blame, a way of making things easier on oneself.",
      "So the next time the words \"they're all like that\" come to our lips, let's stop for a second and ask: How many people have I actually seen? Do they really represent the whole group? Is it right for me to pass judgment on the whole group because of them? Or am I looking at a single leaf and passing judgment on the whole tree?",
      "Every person has their own reckoning. Every mistake belongs to the one who made it. So does every good deed. A label stuck on a group in truth fits no one, because there is no such thing as a group, only individual human beings, each on their own path, in their own struggle."
    ].join('\n\n')
  },
  '3': {
    title: "What I've Come to Understand at 28",
    body: [
      "I'm about to turn 28. Considering that men live 71 years on average, I have already lived 40% of my life. Yet my interest in life keeps growing, because the interesting things that happen to me are helping me understand so much. One of my students once wrote on their blog that what really worried them was growing used to living in this world, and those words made me think very hard. So let me share with you what I have learned during the years I have lived without growing used to living.",
      "What I have understood in the last 2-3 years is many times more than everything I understood before the age of 25.",
      "That is why today I will try to explain what I have come to realize by this age. It will be long, and I hardly need to point out that patient people reach success more often than others.",
      "## Friends",
      "At first, I used to call people friends if they joked around with me and pulled me into all kinds of entertainment (computer games, phone games, videos, movies, talking about girls, gossip). Later I realized that these were not real friends.",
      "A real friend, it turns out, is someone who calls you toward useful things (morning exercise, seeking knowledge, prayer, the *tarawih* night prayers of Ramadan, fasting, doing good, visiting the sick, attending funeral prayers, and so on).",
      "Pay close attention to where the people who call themselves your friends invite you most often.",
      "Run them through this simple filter, and you may find you don't even have as many friends as you have fingers. Now begin to be that kind of friend yourself. Those who are worthy of such friendship will walk with you; those who aren't, won't.",
      "People who score \"points\" by laughing at you in front of others don't know what a real friend is. They are trying to raise their own standing by tearing down yours in front of others. But isn't that exactly what an enemy does?",
      "## Family",
      "Apart from your parents, no one needs you if you are of no use to them. We all know that the people we must value first are our parents. But how should we value them? Simply by sitting and talking with them for a while, simply by hugging and kissing them, simply by receiving their blessing before leaving the house, simply by telling them that we love them (I myself struggle to do this).",
      "When it comes to starting a family, marrying someone only for their figure and body, or, for women, marrying a man for his car and his house, will bring you to the edge of a deep cliff. I've found that the best thing is to talk with the person you want to marry beforehand while you are in different roles. For example, you as the teacher and they as your student, you as the manager and they as an employee, both of you as students, you as a client and they as a buyer, and so on. But in the roles of future groom and future bride, you both hide your shortcomings and faults from each other and come across as perfect people, and in the end your hopes are dashed. When expectations don't match reality, disappointment follows.",
      "I have seen women who constantly go through their husband's phone while locking their own behind three passwords. Out of jealousy over a woman he exchanged a couple of greetings with in messages, such a wife will pack up and go back to her parents' home. But I have also heard of women who never once left for their parents' home, even though their husband openly cheated on them and paid them no attention, even though when they met him after work with \"Welcome home, my dear, did you have a good day?\" he would curse at them, \"What's it to you?\", even though they had to bake fresh hot bread every morning and were forced to go out and work as day laborers.",
      "I have heard of women who left because their husband brought home cheap baby cereal for their child. And I have heard of women who never left their home at all, even when their mother-in-law told them, \"You're not giving your child any baby cereal.\" I have seen women who are never happy no matter what their husband brings, and I have seen women who are as sincerely happy over a single ball of *qurt* (dried salted curd) as if you had bought them a car.",
      "I have seen women who can find a reason for divorce out of nowhere. And I have heard of women who, even with 100 reasons to divorce, find 1 reason to stay and keep living together.",
      "## The Workplace",
      "Since I have spent very little time working as an employee, I am writing from the manager's side.",
      "I have seen employees who kept trying to get a raise using trivial things as an excuse, and I have seen employees whose character is so good that it makes you want to keep raising their salary.",
      "I have seen employees who seem like very good people in their words and actions, but their reactions keep giving away what is really in their hearts. In front of you, they speak with a hand respectfully on their chest; when you are not around, they fling their name badge aside and say, \"So we have to go around wearing this saddle blanket, do we?\" And there are employees who wear their badge with pride, even out on the street.",
      "There are employees whose knowledge is strong, but it has not reached their heart. The knowledge has not taken root in them yet. And there are employees whose knowledge is limited, but whose hearts are pure.",
      "There are employees who will put up with 10 of your bitter moments, and a single sweet word from you lifts them to the sky; you can rely on them in the long run. And there are employees who are pleased by 10 of your sweet moments but cannot bear a single bitter one. You can't rely on people like that in the long run.",
      "There are employees who trust you even when they can't see that you are right, and there are employees who don't trust you even when they can see that you are right.",
      "There are employees who shout, demanding justice, yet when you show them what justice looks like, they cannot accept it.",
      "There are employees who own up to their mistakes, fix them, or at least try to fix them. And there are employees who look for mistakes in others and always put the blame on someone else.",
      "I once read a story in which even Umar ibn al-Khattab, a man regarded as a symbol of justice, made a mistake by reaching a conclusion after hearing only one side. As a leader, I also make a great many mistakes, and yet many employees expect perfection from me.",
      "There are employees who saw me from the outside and expected me to be perfect; when they got to know me more closely and learned that I am just an ordinary servant of God who makes mistakes, their expectations didn't match reality, they were disappointed, and their respect for me fell. But in exactly these situations, there are also staff members who help me correct my mistakes, and from whom I often seek advice on how to improve myself.",
      "## Faith",
      "I began to truly live in 2025. That was the year I finally managed to establish my daily prayers fully, though I don't know whether I established them correctly. But I am trying to get them right. Later I realized that what had been holding me back was a lack of knowledge. I'm the one who keeps saying that ignorance lies at the root of every problem, and yet here I had forgotten that very thing.",
      "I am trying to instill this in my students quickly too, so that they start praying sooner. Somewhere their knowledge is also falling short, and because of that they keep postponing it and losing precious time.",
      "Imagine the Angel of Death, Azrael, came and said, \"I will take your soul in 3 days.\" For those 3 days you would be in constant supplication, prayer, remembrance of Allah (*dhikr*) and repentance. Yet we live as though we had a guarantee that we won't die in 3 days. We live as though Azrael had promised us, \"I will take your soul when you are 70.\"",
      "After all, the very first thing we will be called to account for after death is our prayer.",
      "\"But my work, my studies and all sorts of other troubles keep getting in the way of my prayers,\" you might say. A beautiful answer to this comes from the blog of one of our female staff members: The hypocrite steps out into the street worried about his stomach; the believer steps out worried about his prayer. (That sentence tore my heart to pieces.)",
      "A person who says \"I'm not ready yet\" doesn't actually know what it is they aren't ready for. (Girls who want to start wearing the hijab also somehow think they aren't ready.) It is their lower self (*nafs*) that tells them this: \"You're still young, enjoy yourself a little longer; later you can repent and start praying.\" For that, you would need to get permission from Azrael. In truth, it is the *nafs* that isn't ready. The *nafs* is rebelling.",
      "When the command came to cover the *awrah* (the parts of the body that must be covered), the believing women obeyed it at once. According to the hadiths, after the verses were revealed, the women immediately took their outer cloaks (*jilbab*) and began going about fully covered, or at the very least covered themselves with whatever cloth they could lay their hands on. They did not say \"I'm not ready yet,\" \"as long as our hearts are pure, that's enough,\" or \"it's a big responsibility.\" Dear women, don't be fooled by the tricks of your *nafs*.",
      "The medical benefits of prayer have also been identified.",
      "Praying helps improve the ability to concentrate. What modern language calls meditation is something our own prayer already contains. Regular prayer is good not only for spiritual health but for mental health as well. It helps sharpen concentration, reduce stress and improve overall well-being.",
      "Above, I have briefly mentioned its benefits in this world. Below is a hadith about its benefit in the Hereafter:",
      "The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) gave the following example to explain the importance of prayer in a person's life:",
      "\"If there were a river at the door of one of you, and he bathed in it five times every day, would any dirt remain on his body?\"",
      "The Companions said, \"No, no dirt at all would remain.\"",
      "Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said, \"The five daily prayers are just like that. Through them, Allah washes away sins.\"",
      "## Health",
      "Let me share Shaha Dolimov's examples:",
      "If you were given a Ferrari, how would you take care of it? Of course, you would look after it carefully every day, fill it with the best fuel and the best oil, and try to drive it without letting any harm come to it.",
      "Your body is exactly the same, yet we fill it with the worst fuel (fizzy drinks, Coke, Pepsi, energy drinks) and the worst oil (fast food, junk food), and once the tank is full we keep pouring until it floods the inside of the car. We would wash a Ferrari every day, but we don't give our bodies their daily wash (physical exercise).",
      "Our bodies are made for movement; if you stay in one place without moving, your arms and legs go numb.",
      "Cigarettes, *nos* (oral snuff), vodka: just don't start, end of story. Once you start, there's no way to stop. Because I have kept away from these things since I was young, my classmates know I don't like them, so they do these things away from me. At the very least, they don't invite me. But the pressure on those who used to drink and have now quit is enormous. \"You used to drink, you drunk, so now you've turned into a decent person, have you?\" they say, and force them to drink. A person with plenty of male \"pride\" but weak faith as a believer gives in to these words and goes back to the old drinking. If he had never started, there would be no such talk. If he had never started, seeing others smoke would not set off his craving.",
      "## Goals and Success",
      "Two questions:",
      "Why do we put in the same effort as everyone else, yet want results that not everyone has been able to achieve?",
      "Why do we copy the looks, hair and clothes of successful people, but not their habits, their actions, or the knowledge they acquired?",
      "Your future depends on what you do today. Don't say, \"I'll work hard today and get results right away\"; wait for the harvest season. In 4th grade I started going to English classes, and people said, \"What's the point? He's just a little kid.\" I kept going to a tutor without a break, and they said, \"What's the point? Unless he pays his way into university, it's no use anyway.\" I didn't need a diploma; I needed knowledge. Those who looked down on me when I was young and studying, Allah Himself brought low. I am only now reaping the harvest; the work began long ago.",
      "The biggest obstacle on the road to a goal is fear. If your dreams don't scare you, they aren't big enough. Everyone has fear; what divides people into the successful and the unsuccessful is simply whether they were able to stand up to their fear or not. So if you want to win, first conquer yourself (your fear).",
      "To get what others only dream of, do what others won't do. Only the one who does what others haven't done becomes the winner. If you want to win, stop living like everyone else. What is everyone else doing? 5-6 hours of PUBG a day, or 4-5 hours on Instagram, pointless chats on Telegram, roaming the streets to show off (\"risovka\") for no reason, playing the \"street kid,\" hanging out with classmates every week; everyone is running away from boredom. Don't do that. Learn to be bored, get rid of the games, delete Instagram, put a limit on Telegram. Because if you want to change your life, you first have to change the way you think. Yes, if you are different from everyone else, everyone will see you as a different kind of person, and that is how you get different results.",
      "Set a yearly goal. Because yearly goals are big, break them down into monthly goals, those into weekly goals, and those into daily habits. Make your daily habits easy enough that you'll actually do them. Keep track of your habits by writing them down.",
      "## Character",
      "1. Have clear values. Values are the things you hold dear. For example, if you value honesty, then honesty is one of your values.",
      "2. Principles (rules) grow out of values. From our value of honesty above comes the principle \"I will never lie.\"",
      "3. Work all of this out and write it down for yourself. This is the stage of getting to know yourself.",
      "Examples of the principles of a strong character:",
      "- not making decisions while emotional",
      "- trying to learn something every day",
      "- always trying to look at a situation as a third person would. We usually make wrong decisions because we look at events only from our own side. Being an impartial person yourself, looking at things from above, and judging fairly.",
      "- It is not the person who makes mistakes who fails, but the one who doesn't learn from them",
      "- It is possible to avoid mistakes. For that, it's wise to take the advice of those who have already made them. But if you make the mistake yourself, you learn better",
      "- Not passing on to others what you heard from someone",
      "- In hard times, taking the responsibility on your own shoulders instead of ducking for cover: that is leadership",
      "- Expecting nothing from anyone; one should place hope in Allah alone. Never asking anyone other than Allah for the same thing twice.",
      "- Admitting your mistake and trying to correct it",
      "- Just because your forefathers did something doesn't mean they did it right. The forefathers of the Quraysh tribe also worshipped idols. Check for yourself.",
      "- accept praise, but look at it with doubt. Accept criticism, and look at it as though it holds some truth.",
      "The most dangerous person is the one who doesn't understand himself. Because he doesn't understand himself, he makes everyone suffer. He isn't curious about who he is or what he is like. For us, though, other people's lives, their mistakes and their achievements are more interesting than our own. We enjoy discussing others more than examining ourselves.",
      "We run away from ourselves; we are afraid of getting to know ourselves. We don't learn to understand ourselves. All of this comes from ignorance, from not reflecting, from not breaking events down into causes and consequences, and it just keeps growing.",
      "## The Heart",
      "My heart used to ache over people who left me in situations that were their own fault, where justice was not on their side. One of our staff members left us because we hadn't given them a gift on Teachers' Day, but in fact they weren't a teacher. And I, since it was Teachers' Day, had bought gifts only for the teachers. Even here I counted myself to blame, and my heart suffered terribly. The truth is, I am deeply afraid of upsetting anyone. Even when someone has done something seriously wrong, if I punish them and they get upset, my heart suffers. As a leader facing all sorts of situations, I used to brood a lot, telling myself \"I don't know anything,\" finding fault with myself in every situation, interrogating myself and hurting my own heart. Recently I read about the years of Umar ibn al-Khattab's caliphate and discovered that Umar, too, had lived through the things I had been through; for each of his trials there was a matching one in my own life. Seeing that even Umar, the very embodiment of justice, made mistakes, and that there were even people who, unhappy with his justice, left for the lands of Rum (Byzantium) and turned from Islam to Christianity, helped me, an ordinary person, to be more resolute in such situations and to see them more clearly. In truth, I am very soft-hearted, and I don't make a good leader or manager. I am more of a creator; I love making things.",
      "When someone benefits from me and thanks me, I don't know how to accept the thanks. For some reason I feel unworthy of it. I think it was simply something that was meant to happen, Allah's decree, and I was only the means. But sometimes the person thanking me sees that I don't react and gets hurt, and there are dear students of mine who wonder why their teacher is like this and tell me, their teacher, that they don't like this trait of mine. I don't know how to thank people myself either. When someone gives me a gift, for some reason I'm not very delighted, and when they don't, I'm not upset either. In fact, this comes from my effort not to expect anything from anyone. But if that's the case, shouldn't I be happy? My expectation is zero, and reality brings something better than that. I don't know; I couldn't figure it out myself. Have I forgotten how to be happy? \"You've forgotten how to be grateful,\" one of my students advised me. (Of course, I take advice from my students too.) I denied it and said no, but then I realized that I had not been grateful in my heart. If I slowly begin to be grateful in my heart, perhaps I will learn to be happy. In short, matters of the heart are like this: hard to understand. That is why, after all this time, I still haven't been able to digest Imam al-Ghazali's \"The Key to Hearts.\" I will try reading it again; truth be told, I hadn't even read half of it.",
      "## Books",
      "As I've said before, just as medicine is prescribed according to the illness, you read books according to what ails you. Sometimes, instead of taking in the many things a great book has to offer, I end up just trying to finish it. That is something completely different from finishing a book because you were gripped by it. When I'm trying to finish, I race past the very lines that call for deep reflection, the ones you should sit with and crack open to get at the kernel. Why do I try so hard to finish? Maybe I'm in a hurry to post on my channel, \"Here, I've finished my 15th book\"? Not always, but sometimes, yes.",
      "Even so, reading is the very best habit I have started in the last 3 years. When I face very difficult questions, even ones where, as I hear the question, I'm 100% sure I won't be able to answer, the answers come pouring out after it. I believe that, with Allah's inspiration, books are the means behind this.",
      "When people hear that I haven't read even 100 books, they think I'm joking. It's just that they haven't read even 10, while I have read 5-6 times more than they have. The difference is very noticeable. Knowledge works like that: a little more effort, and the gap keeps growing wider. Aziz Rahimov didn't say for nothing that asymmetric actions bring asymmetric results. The difference in driving between someone with 1 year behind the wheel and someone with 5 years isn't that big. Even the difference between a driver with 1 year of experience and one with 20 years isn't that big. But with knowledge, it is. You read about what you have seen in life, and you see in life what you have read. Every time, what you learn connects to what you have read and seen before. Each time the connections multiply, speeding up like a geometric progression. So if you have read 60 books, people think you have read 1000.",
      "## Writing",
      "I love writing. That is why I have written a lot, and I write on my blog. My thinking becomes clearer. I come to understand myself. I check whether what I have written is right, whether my thinking is right. I pay close attention while writing, and that skill grows. The most important skill today is the ability to pay attention.",
      "I read written blogs; I follow the blogs of our staff and my students. Rather than pictures, poems, songs or videos, I read through their texts. I look to see whether there is any text there you could still benefit from rereading 10 years later, and if there isn't, I leave.",
      "Reading text strengthens your imagination. Writing clears your mind by getting out all the scattered thoughts and daydreams that keep it occupied; it reduces stress and helps you put your thoughts in order.",
      "I have quite a few pieces I have written like this one. I simply wanted to share this one. So that you can start writing too, I recommend the Obsidian app."
    ].join('\n\n')
  },
  '4': {
    title: "Learn to Listen to the Other Person",
    body: [
      "I once wrote a post on whether women should drive cars or not. In it, I put forward the idea that I don't know whether they should drive, but that, like swimming, it is a skill worth learning, because one day it may be needed. After that, a man left a comment on it.",
      "> Man: I have read scholars' answers explaining that driving does not suit a woman's nature. Besides, when a woman drives, the eyes of other male drivers fall on her. In this matter, it is better for a woman to be served by her *mahram* men (her husband or close male relatives).",
      "Here the man's view is this: a woman may drive, but as a precaution, it is better and preferable that she does not.",
      "> Woman: Then why do you think women in Saudi Arabia have been allowed to drive?",
      "The woman who replied to him, without fully reading or understanding his comment, immediately writes, \"Why shouldn't she drive? Look, even Saudi Arabia has allowed it.\" But the man above never said it wasn't allowed.",
      "> Man: I didn't say it's not permissible. They say that, from the standpoint of what is preferable, it's better not to drive. These aren't my words; they are the scholars'. (You can check this on the website \"Zikr ahlidan so'rang.\") What's more, this is not about depriving a woman of anything; on the contrary, it is said out of honor for her. The Sharia is not made up only of \"allowed or not allowed,\" \"haram or halal.\" There are also questions of what is preferable.",
      "So the man, right at the start of his reply, explains his view once more: \"I didn't say it's not permissible.\" In other words, she may drive, but out of honoring our women and wanting to cherish and protect them, it is preferable that they don't. He never said \"she must 100% not drive.\" He is saying that, where there is no necessity, it is better and preferable not to drive, and that this is not his own opinion but the opinion of Islamic scholars.",
      "> Man: One day the students of Imam Azam (Abu Hanifa) saw him cleaning a tiny bit of impurity that had gotten on his clothes, even though it was within the amount that is excused. (Depending on whether the impurity on one's clothing is thick or liquid, Imam Azam had given a fatwa on how much of it is excused if a person prays in it.) When his students asked why he was cleaning it himself after giving a fatwa that this amount is excused, he said: \"That is fatwa; this is taqwa.\"",
      "Beyond that, the man gives a beautiful example for his view and explains the difference between fatwa and taqwa. That is:",
      "Haram: things that are 100% forbidden. If you do them, there is sin; whoever abstains from them earns a reward.",
      "Halal (*joiz*, permissible): if you do it, there is reward; if you don't, there is no sin.",
      "Fatwa: a legal permission or ruling given by a jurist or scholar on the basis of the Sharia. It answers the question \"is it allowed or not?\" But a fatwa is always given on the basis of the \"minimum obligation.\"",
      "For example:",
      "Question: \"May a woman drive a car?\"",
      "Fatwa: \"It is permissible; it is not haram.\"",
      "But seen through the lens of taqwa: \"The better way is to go out with a mahram man.\"",
      "Taqwa: being careful to avoid not only sin but also anything doubtful, and living a pure life in the fear of Allah. It is the stage above the fatwa.",
      "THIS IS WHAT THE MAN WAS TRYING TO SAY AND EXPLAIN.",
      "But the woman still replies without understanding, without analyzing, without thinking it through.",
      "> Woman: Recently a friend and I called the number of the Muslim Board of Uzbekistan where you can ask questions, and asked whether a woman should not ride a horse. They answered that neither the Quran nor the hadith says she must not ride, so she may ride; she only has to set her intention right. If the purpose is to show off to others, then it is not permissible, but with the right purpose she may ride. I think we can draw a conclusion by treating the horse as equal to the car.",
      "The same idea is still lodged in the woman's head: he's saying it isn't allowed, but it is allowed, look, there's no ban on riding a horse, and a horse is a means of transport, just like a car.",
      "My dear sister, try to understand, not to reply. The person answering you never said \"a woman may not drive a car.\" He even wrote it himself: \"I didn't say it's not permissible.\"",
      "The two of you are saying the same thing. There is a difference in just one spot:",
      "The main difference: the \"measure of taqwa\" versus the \"measure of intention.\"",
      "- The man's main measure: taqwa and the protection of modesty.",
      "- The woman's main measure: intention and social need.",
      "These two viewpoints are not opposed; rather, they complement each other.",
      "In the Sharia, too, \"intention\" (innamal a'malu binniyat, \"actions are judged by intentions\") and \"taqwa\" (va libosu-t-taqvo zalika xayr, \"and the garment of taqwa, that is best\") complement each other.",
      "Conclusion: it is not haram for a woman to drive a car, and the evidence is that the sacred texts contain no explicit prohibition.",
      "However, scholars have sometimes recommended not driving as the preferable path, as a precaution. That is a level of taqwa, not a requirement.",
      "In today's circumstances, if a woman drives out of necessity, for safety, or to live an independent life, it is permissible.",
      "If it is done out of arrogance, to show off, or to stir up temptation (*fitna*), then it becomes morally objectionable.",
      "In fact, this exchange reveals the fine line between what is \"permissible\" and what is \"preferable.\"",
      "Permissible: the minimum obligation, the level at which a fatwa is given.",
      "Preferable: the maximum level of taqwa.",
      "There's another conversation very much like this one. Shall I break it down for you the same way? What do you say?"
    ].join('\n\n')
  },
  '5': {
    title: "A 52-Year-Old Father",
    body: [
      "Today is my dad's birthday. So I tried to write an essay, reflecting a little on the place my dad holds in my life.",
      "My dad is someone who has been an example to me both in his words and in his deeds. He didn't just give advice like \"don't drink, don't smoke\"; he himself kept away from drinking and smoking. He showed, in his words and in his deeds alike, that he didn't care for people who drink and smoke.",
      "And that gave me a big push toward one of the best decisions of my life: never going anywhere near drinking or smoking. I remember that when only two of us in our class chose not to drink, everyone pushed us to drink. \"If you're a man, you'll drink,\" \"If you don't drink, you're no man,\" \"If you're my friend, drink for me\": with lines like these, many tried to lead me down that road. Only men understand what kind of pressure this is. An attack on his manhood is the hardest thing a man can face.",
      "Fortunately, the values that had been instilled deep in my heart didn't crack in the slightest. What gave me the strength to answer, \"If not drinking this means I can't be your friend, then fine, we're not friends,\" was this very value my dad had planted in my heart.",
      "My dad doesn't just preach \"do sports\" with empty words. At 52 he can still do the splits; he can do more pull-ups than his 28-year-old son; physically, he is stronger than most unmarried 18-year-old lads; he can shift heavy loads wherever he wants with both his wits and his strength; and he is so nimble he can get up onto a roof without a ladder. So what else would you expect from the son of a man like that?",
      "The person to look up to lives right beside me, after all; I see him every day. He lived a healthy life and showed it to me. Even at his age he carries no extra fat and has no belly, he is lean and wiry, and I still haven't managed to become a son worthy of him.",
      "I wondered how my dad could be so strong and healthy at 52. The answer didn't take long to find. It was my grandfather. When I was at school, we made *sumalak* (a traditional spring dish of sprouted wheat) at home. For the sumalak we had to split and chop firewood. My 70-year-old grandfather looked tougher, stronger and sturdier at splitting wood than the 16- and 17-year-old boys. Even my classmates admitted it.",
      "Just picture it: at 70, swinging a heavy axe around in his hands, splitting firewood. My dad is the son of such a man, and I am the grandson of such a man. The people who inspire me to be just as healthy in my fifties and seventies, God willing, are without a doubt my dad and my grandfather.",
      "Here are other things my dad stresses almost every day: don't lie; don't take what is rightfully someone else's; if you made a promise, keep it; don't laugh at people or make fun of them; if what is yours stays with someone else, let it stay, but never let what belongs to someone else stay with you.",
      "That last line made me think a great deal: if what is yours ends up with someone else, so be it, but never let what belongs to another stay with you.",
      "Behind this advice, it turns out, lies a concern for the distant Hereafter. In the Hereafter, if someone else's due remains with us, we will have to answer for it.",
      "I only came to understand this when I learned that a great many people teach their children the very opposite. Many, it turns out, teach: don't hand over what's yours to anyone, don't let your due stay with someone else, even if someone else's stays with you. They give this advice with this world in mind. That is, simply to come out ahead in this world.",
      "I deeply admire my father's simplicity, his trusting nature, the fact that there isn't a speck of malice in his heart, and the way he throws himself wholeheartedly into anything he takes on. He is quick to anger, and when he gets angry, he gets angry wholeheartedly; at that moment you are the worst person in the world. He is just as quick to be happy, wholeheartedly happy, and when he is happy, you are the best person in the world. My grandfather was the same.",
      "My dad turned his hobby into his work. He loves his work and truly enjoys it. Maybe that is why he never gets tired.",
      "Who in your life rejoices at your success even more than you do? Your father.",
      "Who grieves over the hardships and misfortunes in your life even more than you do? Your father.",
      "My dad is the kind of man who, when he brings something home for our family, for me, for my sisters, for my mom, is happier than when he buys something for himself.",
      "If we say we will buy him new clothes or anything else he needs, he says, \"No, the old ones will do for me.\" Yet he always wants the very best for us, his children, and never lets us say \"this will do.\"",
      "My dad is such a soft-hearted, simple man that he expects no harm from anyone. He always sees the good side.",
      "My dad is the kind of man who often expresses his love not with words but with hard work. Every one of his actions shows love and care. He may not say \"I love you all,\" but what he does shows it.",
      "My dad is the kind of man who carries all of life's responsibilities on his shoulders, yet he doesn't complain or grumble.",
      "Ahh, if I tried to say it all, it wouldn't fit in one post... However much I say about him, it is too little... However much good I do for him, it is not enough...",
      "To close, give this song a listen: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xqj5YTZacNs"
    ].join('\n\n')
  },
  '6': {
    title: "Form and Substance",
    body: [
      "Friends, today let's talk about form and substance.",
      "When you pick up a walnut, the first thing you see is its hard shell. Unless you crack that shell, you can't reach the kernel inside. Yet many people hold on to the shell and never get to the kernel. Sometimes they even mistake the shell itself for the kernel.",
      "The same mistake repeats itself in many areas of life: we pay attention to form and forget substance.",
      "## Exams and Language",
      "Take English. CEFR, IELTS, TOEFL, Linguaskill: these are all forms of examination. Each has its own format and its own scoring system. But the substance is one and the same: each of them tests your level of English.",
      "A person who knows English deeply will score high on whichever exam they sit, because they paid attention to substance, not form. On the other hand, a person who has memorized a single exam format, trained only for that format, but never studied the language in depth will struggle the moment the format changes. They know the shell well, but they have never come to know the kernel.",
      "## The Person and the Heart",
      "It is exactly the same when we get to know people. Appearance, clothing, polished speech, first impressions: all of these are form. The substance lies in the heart. Our Prophet, peace be upon him, said:",
      "> Allah does not look at your outward forms or your wealth; rather, He looks at your hearts and your deeds.",
      "Someone who dresses well and speaks beautifully may carry corrupt intentions inside. Someone who dresses simply and says little may carry a heart of gold. To be taken in by form and fail to see substance is one of the greatest mistakes we can make.",
      "## Dates and Lessons",
      "Take history as a school subject; it has the same problem. At school, students are made to memorize dates: such-and-such an event happened in such-and-such a year. That is form. The substance is something else: what lesson we can draw from that event, why people in that era made the decisions they did, and how it connects to the present day.",
      "A student who has memorized the dates but drawn no lessons has not learned history; they have only collected information about history. By the next year they will have forgotten the dates, because when there is no kernel in the mind, the shell does not stay either.",
      "## Buildings and Knowledge",
      "The same is true of school buildings. A modern building, beautiful furniture, new equipment: all of these are form. The substance is just one thing: is the child actually learning or not?",
      "The finest building can house the weakest education. The plainest room can give the deepest knowledge. Parents often choose a school by its form: what the building looks like, what the yard is like, how many children sit in a class. They pay little attention to substance (the teacher's skill, the depth of the curriculum, what the child is truly learning).",
      "## The Paper and the Professional",
      "The same mistake repeats itself with diplomas. A diploma was meant to be the outward form of knowledge. But for many people, the diploma itself has become the goal. Paying money for a diploma, passing an exam by \"arrangement\": all of this is like buying the shell.",
      "The paper says \"engineer,\" yet its holder doesn't know how to hold a tool. The paper says \"teacher,\" yet its holder can't explain a single topic to a child. Sooner or later employers realize this too: a diploma is form, not substance. And so it does not guarantee the kernel.",
      "## The Wedding and the Blessing",
      "We see the same problem at weddings. A grand wedding feast (*to'y*), crowds of guests, expensive dishes: all of this is form, and it leaves people with the impression of \"a wedding done well.\" But the substance was meant to be something else, wasn't it? The whole purpose of a wedding was for two young people to be happy, wasn't it?",
      "Because we forgot substance and focused on form, we lost the harmony between two families, the openness between bride and groom, and the blessing on the home. How many families have we seen go into debt to throw a big wedding, then spend years paying it off? On the wedding day the form was perfect, but the kernel (peace in the family) was filled with worries about debt from the very first years.",
      "## The Picture and the Life",
      "Social media is exactly the same. Beautiful photos, the look of a happy family, the image of a successful life: all of these are form. The substance is the real life that stays behind the camera.",
      "Someone may be smiling in a photo while struggling inside. Someone may display a \"free and happy life\" while actually drowning in debt. The viewer, meanwhile, sees the form, compares themselves to it, and feels inadequate and unlucky. In truth, both of them are looking at the shell: one is hiding the kernel, and the other is suffering without knowing that the kernel isn't there.",
      "## Titles and Service",
      "It is the same with positions and careers. Words like \"director,\" \"professor\" and \"mentor\" (*ustoz*) are the outward form of being worthy of respect. The substance is something else: does that person truly serve, do they feel the weight of responsibility, do they bring benefit to those around them?",
      "A society that honors people for their titles is often deceived, because a title is easier to buy than skill.",
      "## Khidr and Moses",
      "The Quran, too, contains a story that shows the difference between form and substance. In Surah al-Kahf we find the story of Khidr, peace be upon him, and Moses, peace be upon him. Khidr makes a hole in a boat, kills a boy, and straightens and repairs a crumbling wall. Outwardly (that is, in form), all of these look like bad deeds, even injustice. Moses, too, looks at that form and objects.",
      "But in the end the substance is revealed: the hole in the boat saved it from a tyrant king. The boy's death protected his parents from evil in the future. The repaired wall preserved the hidden treasure of orphaned children.",
      "Form, the deed we can see, does not fully reveal substance. Judging by form alone often leads us to the wrong conclusion.",
      "## Why Are We Fooled by Form?",
      "Our thoughtful readers will naturally ask: why are we so easily fooled by form?",
      "Because form is easy to see, while substance is hard to find. Judging someone by a certificate is far quicker than testing their real knowledge. Judging by clothes is far easier than knowing a heart. Looking at a building is far quicker than assessing the quality of education. By nature, people choose what is comfortable, not the hard road. That is why so many stop at form.",
      "Besides, form is also used to impress others. A diploma hangs on the wall, and whoever sees it shows respect. A big wedding is held, and people say, \"They did it in style.\" A beautiful photo goes up on social media, and friends feel envious. Form brings quick returns in social status. Substance, by contrast, is built slowly, with patience, and often out of sight.",
      "## So What Should We Do?",
      "First of all, we need to learn to ask ourselves: am I building form, or substance?",
      "- When learning a language: is it for the certificate, or to truly know the language?\n- When earning a diploma: is it for the paper, or to truly become a professional?\n- When holding a wedding: is it to put on a show for people, or for the blessing on the family?",
      "Next, when we judge others, we should remember the same question. Look at a person's work, not their title. Look at how they treat people, not at their clothes. Look at their deeds, not at their photos.",
      "And most importantly, we ourselves must make a habit of tending to the kernel. The kernel is built slowly: quietly, often with no one watching. But a person who has a kernel lives happily even without a shell. A person without a kernel, however beautiful the shell, lives with a sense of emptiness inside.",
      "> Crack a walnut open and see. What is truly valuable never lies on the outside."
    ].join('\n\n')
  },
  '7': {
    title: "You Are No Longer the Average of the Five People Around You",
    body: [
      "Jim Rohn once said: \"You are the average of the five people you spend the most time with.\"",
      "Back when those words were spoken, people spent their time on the street, in the teahouse, or together in one workplace. Today the situation is different. We spend more time not with the people around us but with the faces inside the flat piece of glass in our pockets. That is why the rule has to be revised for our times:",
      "> You are the average of the five blogs you follow.",
      "Recently, while teaching a class of senior students, I witnessed something curious. The conversation turned to worldview, and I gave as examples some of the world's great minds: Charlie Munger, Warren Buffett, Ray Dalio, Alex Hormozi, Sam Altman, Jordan Peterson. Silence. It turned out not one of the students knew any of them. Fine, I thought, across the ocean is a long way off, so I asked about our own hands-on entrepreneurs: Jahongir Ortiqxo'jayev, Murod Nazarov, Zafar Hoshimov. Silence again.",
      "But when I asked, \"Well then, who do you know?\", they answered at once, rattling off a string of names and talking over one another. When I looked into it, these turned out to be social media \"traders\" and hype artists who ride around in rented cars and show you how to make easy money overnight.",
      "So I said to the students: do you see the difference between you and me? The people I follow and the people you follow will also decide the difference in your lives. The reason is simple: whoever you spend the whole day looking at, your mind gradually takes their shape. In the past, a person was shaped by looking up to the teacher or scholar of their own neighborhood (*mahalla*); now people are growing up looking at alien algorithms on a screen.",
      "In Jim Rohn's day, we at least chose those five people ourselves. Today they are often chosen by an algorithm. And an algorithm works not to make you a better person but to keep you on the screen longer: whichever video your finger pauses on, it shows you more of the same tomorrow. It studies not your strongest side but your weakest. There is a well-known saying: if you are not paying for the product, then you are the product. Draw your own conclusions.",
      "We spend months deciding which school and which teacher to entrust our child to, and we ask acquaintances for advice. Yet we often don't even know who the \"teacher\" is who talks into our child's ear for hours every day. A child sits with the teacher at school for 45 minutes, but with the \"teacher\" on the phone, for hours. The question is simple: who is actually raising your child today?",
      "The French philosopher René Girard called this \"mimetic desire\": we do not invent our desires ourselves; we copy them from the people around us. In other words, we are mimetic creatures who want whatever those around us want. Whatever our eyes fall on most often, our hearts lean toward.",
      "When our forefathers said, \"Walk with the good and you will reach your goal; walk with the bad and you will be left in shame,\" they meant the person walking at our side. Today, the \"side\" in that proverb is in our pocket. So I suppose that in today's digital environment we will have to rewrite our folk proverbs as well:",
      "- Walk close to Instagram, and your wants will multiply.\n- Show me your browser (or GPT) history, and I'll tell you who you are.\n- A friend treats you by your blog (your thinking); an enemy, by your Instagram account (your looks).\n- Even eyeglasses have cameras.\n- One who can't get enough of Reels won't get enough of Stories either.\n- When a new bride comes into the house, look at her \"screen time\".",
      "Have you ever eaten a spoonful of pure honey? If you drink a cup of ordinary sweet tea right after it, the tea will taste like sugarless, flavorless water. In fact, the tea is as sweet as ever; your tongue has simply taken a hit of overwhelming, intense sweetness a moment earlier. Your sense of normal has been thrown off.",
      "The cheap hype, the flashy rented cars and the luxury paraded on social media have exactly the same effect on our minds. They hit the brain with an artificial, intense jolt of pleasure: \"You can get rich in a single day without working.\" A person who grows used to this fake glitter slowly loses the taste for real work, the kind done gradually, honestly and with patience. Reading books, learning a trade, sweating for years all begin to look pointless and hard. The illusion of easy results is the most dangerous trap of all: it breaks our mental capacity and cuts us off from real life.",
      "Here is another bitter truth: with every minute you spend watching that video, you are paying the rent on that rented car. Views are their income. In other words, we are financing that fake life ourselves, with our own time.",
      "Let's do a little math. Three hours a day on the feed comes to more than a thousand hours a year, or 45 full days and nights. A book takes on average 6 to 8 hours to read. So the time a year of scrolling eats up could hold more than a hundred books. We say, \"I don't have time to read.\" The time is there; it is just going to Instagram.",
      "Just as a person sorts the food that goes into their stomach, they must also sift the information that comes in through their eyes and ears.",
      "To do this sifting, ask yourself three questions, for example: Is the life this person shows real, their own or rented? What is it giving me, knowledge or mere longing? What will I be left with after the video, a desire to act or envy toward someone?",
      "To be fair, I am not against social media. That very same phone brings Munger, Hormozi and our finest teachers into our homes. The problem is not the screen; it is who we keep on that screen. A knife can cut bread, and it can cut a hand. The difference is decided not by the knife but by the hand that holds it.",
      "There are YouTube projects that helped shape my worldview, many of whose episodes I have watched myself (some 70% of them, some 100%), and there are Telegram channels I follow regularly. Watching these channels starting from their very first videos gives a person a solid, complete foundation. I have put the full list at the end of this essay, in the \"Appendix\".",
      "We may not always be able to choose who lives next to us. But whose voice comes in through our earphones, and whose thoughts our eyes are fixed on, is one hundred percent our own decision.",
      "If Jim Rohn were alive today, he might well say: you are the average of the five bloggers you follow. And you are the one who chooses them.",
      "Change the people on your screen. Then your thoughts will change, and after your thoughts, your whole life.",
      "## Appendix: Sources Worth Following",
      "Channels and podcasts worth watching on YouTube:",
      "- [Osmondagi bolalar](https://www.youtube.com/@Osmondagibolalar): real stories of young people who reached great results through hard work, drive and the trials they went through.\n- [Alohida Mavzu](https://www.youtube.com/@Alohidamavzu): just the thing for steering clear of surface-level news and getting to the heart of a single issue.\n- [Asoschilar podcast](https://www.youtube.com/@asoschilar): not theory, but candid analysis from practitioners who have built businesses in real life and learned from their mistakes.\n- [Aziz Rahimov](https://www.youtube.com/@azizrakhimov): a systematic approach to management, methodology and education; a source where every video is worth studying in full.\n- [Millat Umidi](https://www.youtube.com/@UmidjonEshmukhamedov) and [MU master](https://www.youtube.com/@MU_Master): conversations that give a clear goal and direction to those on the path of learning and professional growth.\n- [Maqsad](https://www.youtube.com/@maqsadclub) and [Maqsad klub](https://www.youtube.com/@maqsadhamjamiyati): the discipline of action, personal productivity and the mechanics of getting results.\n- [Shaha Dolimov](https://www.youtube.com/@shahadolimov): lessons in modern skills, international experience and freedom of thought.\n- [Davronbek Turdiyev](https://www.youtube.com/@DAVRONBEKTURDIEV): ways to expand memory, focus and mental capacity.\n- [Alisher Isayev](https://www.youtube.com/@AlisherIsaev): analysis of business processes, sales systems and practical management.\n- [Kapital](https://www.youtube.com/@Kapitall_uz): financial literacy and the laws of economics; lessons that teach you to look at money with your mind, not your emotions.\n- [Ustozation](https://www.youtube.com/@ustozation): deep reflections on pedagogy and modern teaching methods.\n- [Subyektiv](https://www.youtube.com/@SUBYEKTIV): analytical reports that point to the painful spots in our society that not everyone is willing to talk about.\n- [Kalibr](https://www.youtube.com/@Hasan_Mamasaidov): serious conversations with masters of their fields that teach standards and principles.\n- [Jamshidxon Ziyoxonov](https://www.youtube.com/@Ziyokhonov): sincere conversations for developing an analytical outlook and logical thinking.\n- [Xasanxon Yahyo Abdulmajid](https://www.youtube.com/@Hasanxondomla): one of the most trustworthy teachers for understanding our religious and spiritual roots deeply, objectively and with wisdom.\n- [Fikr yetakchilari](https://www.youtube.com/@fikr_yetakchilari): the way of thinking of people who make decisions and are able to influence society.\n- [Gashtak](https://www.youtube.com/@Gashtak): warm, friendly round-table conversations with no staged scripts whatsoever.\n- Edu-podcast: real experiences of practitioners in international education and language learning.",
      "## Telegram: A Quiet Space for Thought",
      "Telegram is free of noise and clamor, the most comfortable place for thinking over a text. Here are curated sources there that are worth reading:",
      "- [@azizrakhimov_blog](https://t.me/azizrakhimov_blog): a corner for education, self-management and practical observations.\n- [@mashrabov_aa](https://t.me/mashrabov_aa): a space for professional experience and objective analysis.\n- [@aqlli_ikkichi](https://t.me/aqlli_ikkichi): thinking outside the standard molds and unconventional approaches.\n- [@boshqaruvchiga](https://t.me/boshqaruvchiga): practical rules for leadership, team building and creating systems.\n- [@xodimga](https://t.me/xodimga): the job market, professional ethics and the principles of working on yourself.\n- [@AlisherIsaev_blogi](https://t.me/AlisherIsaev_blogi): open reflections on business processes and the secrets of management.\n- [@DrBekOlimjon](https://t.me/DrBekOlimjon): analysis of science, health and mental discipline.\n- [@shahadolimov](https://t.me/shahadolimov): useful observations on personal growth and modern skills."
    ].join('\n\n')
  },
  '8': {
    title: "Why Doesn't Anything Interest Me?",
    body: [
      "I hear the same words from many students: \"*Teacher, I'm not interested in anything.*\" Parents put it differently: \"*My child isn't interested in anything. Not books, not sports, not any profession. Only the phone.*\"",
      "If you think this about yourself too, here is some good news first: **this is not a flaw you were born with. You were not born lazy or untalented.** The cause lies elsewhere. And once you understand it, you can change the situation yourself.",
      "The reason is simple: you don't know enough yet. **And interest is born from knowledge.**",
      "This often sounds backwards. We usually think \"first interest, then study.\" But in fact it is the other way around: **first a little knowledge, then interest.**",
      "## In an unfinished puzzle, you can't see the gap",
      "Have you ever put together a jigsaw puzzle? While the puzzle is still in pieces, you don't know which piece is missing. Everything is missing, so no single gap stands out. But once most of the picture is in place and only one piece is left, that gap stares right back at you. You can't rest until you fill it.",
      "![With two pieces, you see neither a picture nor a gap. But when the picture is almost complete, the missing piece jumps out at once: the middle of the house is gone!](/assets/insho/8-jumboq.svg)",
      "In a [study](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.116.1.75) published in 1994, Carnegie Mellon University psychologist George Loewenstein explains curiosity in exactly this way. *Curiosity arises when a person senses a gap between what they know and what they want to know.* Notice this: to sense a gap, you have to know something around it. **A person who knows nothing does not see the gap.** To them, everything is equally empty.",
      "Take football. For someone who doesn't know the rules, 90 minutes is just twenty-two people running after one ball. Boring. For someone who understands football, every move is a question: why did the coach substitute that particular player? Why did the defender push forward? How will this result change the standings? **The game is the same. The difference is in the viewer's head.**",
      "## Knowledge connects like neurons",
      "Our brain does not store knowledge in separate boxes. Every new piece of information connects to something we already know. In his 1949 [study](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hebbian_theory), the Canadian scientist Donald Hebb described it like this: neurons that fire together wire together. In other words, **knowledge grows not like a thread, but like a web.**",
      "This web has one wonderful property: **wherever two things you know connect, a new question is born.** For example, a child knows that dinosaurs lived millions of years ago. Then they learn that traces of bones are found in rocks. The two connect, and a question appears: \"How do they know how old a rock is?\" That question leads them to the layers of the earth, from there to chemistry, and from there to physics. Each connection reveals the next thing you don't know.",
      "![When knowledge is scarce, there are no threads between the dots, and no questions either. As knowledge grows, the dots connect, and a new \"why?\" hangs from every edge of the web.](/assets/insho/8-tarmoq.svg)",
      "In 1988, the American researchers Donna Recht and Lauren Leslie carried out a [study](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-0663.80.1.16). They gave students a text about a baseball game and tested how well they understood it. The result was unexpected: weak readers who knew baseball well understood the text better than strong readers who didn't know baseball. So **when it comes to understanding something new, prior knowledge helps even more than reading skill.**",
      "## The more you know, the more questions you have",
      "Imagine knowledge as a circle. Inside the circle is everything you know; outside it is everything you don't. **The edge of the circle is where questions are born**, because that is exactly where what you know touches what you don't.",
      "![When the island is small, the shore where it touches the ocean is short too. As the island grows, the shoreline gets longer and the questions multiply.](/assets/insho/8-doira.svg)",
      "You can also compare this to an island. There is a saying attributed to the American physicist John Wheeler: \"We live on an island surrounded by a sea of ignorance. As our island of knowledge grows, so does the shore of our ignorance.\" In other words, what you know is the island, and what you don't know is its shore. The bigger the island, the longer its shore, and the more questions there are looking out toward the ocean.",
      "That is why true scholars, the more they learn, say \"it turns out I know nothing.\" A similar saying is attributed to the ancient Greek philosopher Socrates: \"I know only one thing: that I know nothing.\" A person who knows nothing, on the other hand, often feels as if they know everything. Because they have no questions.",
      "This is why **interest does not grow evenly; it grows like an avalanche.** In his 1986 [study](https://doi.org/10.1598/RRQ.21.4.1), the psychologist Keith Stanovich called this phenomenon in reading the \"Matthew effect\": *a child who learns to read well early reads more; because they read more, they know more; and because they know more, reading becomes even easier and more interesting for them. A child who doesn't read, meanwhile, falls further behind year after year. The gap between the two does not shrink with time; on the contrary, it widens.*",
      "## Then why is the phone so interesting?",
      "Now a fair question arises: if interest requires knowledge, then why is the phone equally interesting to every child?",
      "Because **reels are made to require no prior knowledge at all.** You need neither a web nor a hook to understand them. Every video brings its own ready-made interest: quick cuts, an unexpected ending, a laugh.",
      "You can compare this to glutamate in food. Monosodium glutamate is added to quick processed foods like chips, instant noodles and sausage. The moment it touches the tongue, before you even chew, it sends a strong pleasure signal to the brain. Natural meat has the same taste, but to notice it you have to chew the meat for a long time. A tongue used to glutamate then finds ordinary home-cooked food bland. Reels are exactly the same: ready-made interest you get without chewing. A brain used to them then finds the kind of interest you have to chew, that is, a book, a problem to solve, a profession, bland.",
      "Does a reel leave you with a question? Usually not. The next video, then the next one. A good book, on the other hand, leaves a question behind. That is the difference: one is interest without knowledge, the other is interest born from knowledge. **The first takes from you; the second adds to you.**",
      "Artificial intelligence, too, answers any question in an instant. But it cannot give you the question itself. **Only knowledge gives you the question.**",
      "## Don't sit and wait for interest",
      "Now we come to the most important point. If interest is born from knowledge, then at the very beginning, when there is no knowledge yet, there is no interest either. So **the first step is taken not by interest, but by patience and persistence.**",
      "Every student who has started learning English knows this. The first months are boring: letters, words, rules. Nothing makes sense, nothing is interesting. It is not for nothing that language teachers say, \"until you memorize your first thousand words, you will understand almost nothing in the language.\" You can go on for years without memorizing those thousand words, and you will keep on not understanding the language. But one day the thousand words add up. Then you suddenly understand a line in your favorite song. You catch a joke in a film without a translation. And all at once interest appears: \"I wonder what the rest of it says?\" Now no one has to force you. Knowledge itself pulls you in.",
      "![At first there is almost no interest, so pushing and incentives hold things up. After a certain point the lines cross, and interest takes over the load.](/assets/insho/8-grafik.svg)",
      "The left side of the graph is the hardest stage. This is exactly where most people stop and conclude, \"I guess it's just not interesting to me.\" In reality, they simply haven't reached the place where interest appears yet. They are like a passenger who gets off the bus before reaching the stop and says, \"this road doesn't lead anywhere.\"",
      "Parents often make the same mistake: \"If my child isn't interested, I won't force them. If they get interested on their own, they'll study.\" The intention is good, but the conclusion is wrong. **A child who knows nothing will not suddenly become interested in something on their own.** Interest needs a gap, and to see a gap you need knowledge around it. Bringing the child to that first bit of knowledge, that is, getting them through the first \"forcing\" stage, is the job of the adults.",
      "Of course, when I say forcing, I don't mean only strictness. It is about one goal: the child has to get through the first stage, one way or another. There are many ways to do this. Promising a small gift when they finish a book. Letting them collect points for the words they memorize each day and giving a reward at the end of the week. Sitting beside them and reading together, then asking what they learned and praising them. Having them compete with a friend. These rewards do not replace interest; they only carry the child to the point where interest appears. Once they get there, the rewards are no longer needed: knowledge itself pulls the child in.",
      "It is not for nothing that our forefathers said, \"Seeking knowledge is like digging a well with a needle.\" The first scratches of the needle seem to dig nothing at all. But every well begins with exactly those.",
      "## Two loops",
      "Now let's put it all together. There are two loops in life.",
      "![The loop of ignorance spins in place. The loop of knowledge grows with every turn and goes on without end.](/assets/insho/8-halqa.svg)",
      "The first is the loop of knowledge: you know something, a question arises, the question sparks interest, interest makes you study, and studying gives you new knowledge. This loop widens with every turn. The second is the loop of ignorance: you don't know, no question arises, you're not interested, you don't study, and once again you don't know. This loop turns too, but in place. Years go by, and the person stays at the same point.",
      "There is only one way to cross from one loop to the other: **to start learning something even while there is no interest yet.** Choose one field. Learn its most basic foundations, even if you have to force yourself. Every evening, ask yourself: \"What new question came up for me today?\" If questions begin to appear, it means the web is being woven.",
      "**Don't sit and wait for interest; build it with knowledge.** Because a person who just sits and waits can never get out of the loop of ignorance.",
      "> **So no questions ever arise in a child who knows nothing.** With no questions, they are interested in nothing. Not interested, they don't learn. And so they go on, knowing nothing, without knowledge, and remain ignorant."
    ].join('\n\n')
  }
};

/* "Men haqimda" sahifasining inglizcha matni */
window.ABOUT_EN = "<p class=\"about-lead\">Jahongir Ilhomjonovich Zoxidov. I was born on 17 March 1997 in the town of Zafar, Bekobod district, Tashkent region.</p>\n\n      <p>I work in education: as a teacher, entrepreneur, mentor and author. My days are spent among students, teachers and classrooms. Most of the essays on this site came not from books but from experiences in those very places.</p>\n\n      <div class=\"about-facts\">\n        <div class=\"row\"><span class=\"k\">Born</span><span class=\"v\">17 March 1997, Zafar, Bekobod district</span></div>\n        <div class=\"row\"><span class=\"k\">Profession</span><span class=\"v\">Educator, entrepreneur, mentor, author</span></div>\n        <div class=\"row\"><span class=\"k\">Teaching</span><span class=\"v\">Since 2017</span></div>\n        <div class=\"row\"><span class=\"k\">Education</span><span class=\"v\">University of Sunderland (United Kingdom), BA (Hons) Business and Management, 2020</span></div>\n        <div class=\"row\"><span class=\"k\">English</span><span class=\"v\">IELTS 8.0 (Listening 9.0, Reading 9.0), CEFR C1</span></div>\n        <div class=\"row\"><span class=\"k\">Projects</span><span class=\"v\">Jony Academy, Jony School, Stech</span></div>\n        <div class=\"row\"><span class=\"k\">Author</span><span class=\"v\">More than 10 books</span></div>\n      </div>\n\n      <h2>What I write about</h2>\n\n      <p>For many years I wrote my essays on my <a href=\"https://t.me/JahongirZoxidov\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Telegram channel</a>. But Telegram is a place built for quick messaging. Long texts get lost there, and they are hard to read: the scrolling goes on and on, the letters are cramped, and your eyes get tired. That is why I opened my personal website. From now on, this will be the main home for my essays.</p>\n\n      <p>I don't tie myself to a fixed schedule. I write when inspiration comes, when I have gathered something to say. That is why my essays are long.</p>\n\n      <p>Sometimes they run past two thousand words. I would rather open an idea up all the way to the end than leave it half-finished.</p>\n\n      <p>The topics vary: personal growth, psychology, education, family, finance, self-awareness, religion. But they all share one goal: to explain, in simple language, the experiences I have lived through and the hard knocks I have taken along the way. I try not to write about things I haven't lived or felt myself. Some of what I write, and some of my views, may change over time. So I don't claim that everything I write is 100% true. These are simply my thoughts.</p>\n\n      <p>For me, writing is a way of thinking: I think on paper. I have always seen the word \"essay\" as \"an attempt\": you start writing, and ideas start to come. Try it. Once you get a taste for it, you'll stop only when your hand starts to ache.</p>\n\n      <p>If my essays have made you think about something, that alone is enough for me.</p>\n\n      <h2>My books</h2>\n\n      <p>I have written more than 10 books. Most of them are about learning English, preparing for IELTS and the craft of teaching:</p>\n\n      <ul class=\"about-ul\">\n        <li>\"Ingliz tilini o'rganish sanati\" (The Art of Learning English)</li>\n        <li>\"Wow Grammar\"</li>\n        <li>\"Jony Essays\"</li>\n        <li>The IELTS 1.0-7.0 intensive series</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>My projects</h2>\n\n      <p class=\"about-role\"><strong>Jony Academy</strong> is an English language learning center that began in one small room in Bekobod on 19 August 2019. Today it has 5 branches, nearly 70 staff and 750 to 850 students. We prepare students for IELTS and CEFR/Multilevel certificates. I am its founder.</p>\n\n      <p class=\"about-role\"><strong>Jony School</strong> is a private school and my newest project. From September 2026, a new 1,400-square-meter floor will be fully up and running. I am one of its founders.</p>\n\n      <p class=\"about-role\"><strong>Stech edu</strong> is a learning center for modern professions, programming and information technology. I am one of its founders.</p>\n\n      <p class=\"about-role\"><strong>TTP: Ustozlik Psixologiyasi</strong> (The Psychology of Mentorship) is a special course for moving from being a teacher to being an ustoz, a true mentor. I teach it myself. So far, 2 groups have already graduated. I publish my writing on this subject on my <a href=\"https://t.me/ustozlikilmi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\"Ustozlik ilmi\"</a> Telegram channel.</p>\n\n      <p class=\"about-role\"><strong>Shogirdlik dasturi</strong> (Apprenticeship Program) is a special mentorship program for apprentices. I have put everything I know in every field, everything I have lived through, and the lessons I have drawn from my mistakes into one complete course and mentorship format. Only boys are admitted, and only after an interview. I have already seen 2 groups through to graduation.</p>\n\n      <p class=\"about-role\"><strong>Nero Club</strong> is a community that works on family values and heritage. I am a co-founder.</p>\n\n      <p>All of these rest on one foundation: the biggest change in education is made not by a new building or a beautiful curriculum, but by a teacher with strong, well-prepared knowledge. Everything else comes after that.</p>\n\n      <h2>More on my education</h2>\n\n      <p>I finished school in 2012 as a straight-A student, and graduated from college in 2015 with an honors diploma. Then I moved to Tashkent.</p>\n\n      <p>In 2016 I enrolled at MDIST (Management Development Institute of Singapore in Tashkent). The institute awards degrees in partnership with the University of Sunderland in Britain. On 14 July 2020 I received my BA (Hons) Business and Management degree from Sunderland with a 2:1 (Upper Second-Class Honours). The entire program was taught in English and assessed in English.</p>\n\n      <p>My path in English was gradual: IELTS 6.5 (2016), 7.5 (2021), 8.0 (2024). In my latest result, Listening and Reading were both 9.0. In 2022 I also earned a C1 certificate from the State Testing Center. Growing by one and a half bands in eight years is not fast. But so that I would never have to tell a student of mine \"I'll take you to a peak I have never climbed myself,\" I had to walk this road myself.</p>\n\n      <p>Since 2010 I have read nearly 200 books. I try not to leave reading up to my mood, but sometimes it doesn't work out. Without a plan and consistency, a book just stays on the shelf.</p>\n\n      <p>I am not shy about sitting in a course as a student, either. The courses I have completed so far:</p>\n\n      <ul class=\"about-ul\">\n        <li><strong>\"Tizim Markaz\"</strong>: Axrolxo'ja Rahmatxo'jayev's courses on \"Systematizing Learning Centers\" and on the craft of mentorship. I received a certificate as the best student.</li>\n        <li><strong>MFaktor 56 INTENSIVE</strong>: 29 October 2023. An intensive on \"Systematizing and Scaling Learning Centers\" organized by Hasan and Husan Mamasaidov.</li>\n        <li><strong>Aziz Rahimov's apprenticeship program</strong>: in mentorship.</li>\n        <li><strong>Javohir Quvvatov</strong>: a course on the concept of pedagogy.</li>\n        <li><strong>\"Biznesni davolash\"</strong> (\"Curing the Business\"): Alisher Isayev's one-month course at Sales Doctor Academy, 10 May to 1 June 2025. Nearly 300 entrepreneurs took the course, and 7 of them were awarded the diploma. I am one of those seven.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Hobbies and interests</h2>\n\n      <p>I started running in 4th grade. In the long-distance running test in PE class, I was the first to cross the line. I still remember how my classmates' cheering gave me strength when I was exhausted. Until I finished school, I never once lost first place in long-distance running. Then I gave it up for many years.</p>\n\n      <p>In 2025 I came back to running. The races I have completed so far:</p>\n\n      <ul class=\"about-ul\">\n        <li><strong>\"G'olib\" Marathon</strong>: 10 kilometers, 12 January 2025. Time: 1 hour 4 minutes 50 seconds.</li>\n        <li><strong>7th Tashkent International Marathon</strong>: 10 kilometers, 13 April 2025.</li>\n        <li><strong>Samarkand Half Marathon</strong>: 21.1 kilometers, 2025. My first half marathon. Time: 2 hours 34 minutes 56 seconds.</li>\n        <li><strong>Zaamin Ultra</strong>: 21 kilometers on a mountain trail, June 2026. Time: 3 hours 38 minutes 26 seconds.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <p>Climbing mountains is my favorite pastime. The peaks I have climbed so far:</p>\n\n      <ul class=\"about-ul\">\n        <li><strong>Buritau</strong>: 1,295 meters, 6 April 2025.</li>\n        <li><strong>Kichik Chimyon</strong>: about 2,100 meters, 26 August 2025.</li>\n        <li><strong>Katta Chimyon</strong>: 3,309 meters, 1 September 2025. Just six days between Kichik and Katta Chimyon.</li>\n        <li><strong>Abnur</strong>: 2,391 meters, 23 July 2026. The ascent took about 4 hours 30 minutes.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <p>On Katta Chimyon I spent 8 hours going up and 5 hours coming down, and took 38,000 steps. In some places the slope reached 80 degrees, and several people turned back. I didn't think about turning back for a single second.</p>\n\n      <p>All of this taught me one thing: a huge result turns out to be the sum of small but steady steps. From one room to five branches, from 6.5 to 8.0, from 10 kilometers to a half marathon: it all happened this way. We started with a small step and just kept growing, slowly.</p>\n\n      <h2>My roots and the name \"Jony\"</h2>\n\n      <p>My roots, on both my father's and my mother's side, go back to Andijan. The teaching tradition in our family begins with my grandfather. In his day, he was a Russian language teacher well known in the area. My surname comes from his name, so it reminds me of my roots every day. My grandmother, in turn, taught me to play chess in 5th and 6th grade; she was the first to teach me to think patiently and to think ahead.</p>\n\n      <p>I never went to kindergarten. My mother was both my first teacher and my kindergarten teacher: I learned to read and write at home and started school at 6. My father worked honestly all his life in many different jobs, and today he is an independent entrepreneur. The biggest thing I learned from him: do work you truly enjoy, and you will never work a day in your life. My father's own hobby is now his work. He doesn't spend his days working; he spends them on his hobby.</p>\n\n      <p>As a child, my family affectionately called me \"Jony\"; the nickname was given to me by my father's older brother. Years later, when it was time to name my first learning center, I didn't have to think long: Jony Academy. The name was not chosen by chance; it is my roots.</p>";
